  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct evidence (human/animal)**  \n- In mice and human studies, IL‑17‑producing RORγt⁺ CD4⁺ T cells were identified as a distinct helper‑T subset that *promotes autoimmune tissue inflammation*【PMID: 27576555】 (animal‑model and translational evidence).  \n\n**Association (broader literature)**  \n- Multiple reviews cite IL‑17A as a driver of several autoimmune diseases, including multiple sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis, ankylosing spondylitis, and inflammatory bowel disease【PMID: 37670700】【PMID: 30891627】, indicating a consistent association across diverse disease contexts.  \n\n**Testable hypothesis**  \n- *If IL‑17 signaling is blocked in early‑stage autoimmunity, then the differentiation of pathogenic Th17‑derived GM‑CSF⁺/IFN‑γ⁺ effector cells will be reduced, attenuating disease severity* (derived from the described IL‑23‑driven pathogenic cascade in intestinal Th17 cells【PMID: 34875227】).  \n\n**Study‑context limitations**  \n1. **Model reliance** – The primary mechanistic data come from mouse models of experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis, collagen‑induced arthritis, and other induced diseases; human‐specific pathways may differ.  \n2. **Review‑based sources** – Several cited records are narrative reviews that synthesize primary studies without presenting new experimental data, limiting direct causal inference.  \n3. **Pleiotropic role of IL‑17** – IL‑17 can be protective (e.g., antibacterial immunity) and pathogenic, so blockade may have off‑target effects not captured in the cited disease‑focused reports.  \n4. **Heterogeneity of IL‑17 sources** – Innate cells (γδ T, NKT, ILC3) also produce IL‑17; the cited evidence does not always separate adaptive versus innate contributions, complicating interpretation of therapeutic targeting.  \n\n**Graph‑derived candidate insights**  \n\n- **GRAPH‑CONFIRMED** decd42689d2018ff: il-17 promotes autoimmune disease【PMID: 27576555】  \n\n*These insights echo the direct experimental observations and reinforce the causal link between IL‑17 activity and autoimmune pathology.*  \n\n**Conclusion** – Experimental and translational data consistently support IL‑17 as a promoter of autoimmune disease, but most evidence derives from animal models and review syntheses, necessitating careful validation in human cohorts and consideration of IL‑17’s dualistic immune functions."
  },
